# CHOWN(1)

## NAME

chown, chgrp - change owner or group

## SYNOPSIS

| **chown** owner file ...

| **chgrp** group file ...

## DESCRIPTION

*Chown* changes the owner of the *files* to *owner*. The owner may be either a decimal UID or a
login name found in the password file.

*Chgrp* changes the group-ID of the *files* to *group*. The group may be either a decimal GID
or a group name found in the group-ID file.

Only the super-user can change owner or group, in order to simplify as yet unimplemented
accounting procedures.

**Note:** that is enforced by the kernel and not by these programs. Chown(2) here opens with

> **if (!suser() || (ip = owner()) == NULL) return;**

\- kernel/sys4.c - so an ordinary user is refused whatever the file's mode says, and both
programs are the super-user's alone. Neither is a set-user-ID program, and neither should
become one: the whole point of the rule is that a user cannot give a file away.

**Note:** *chgrp* has no manual page of its own and no source beyond cmd/chgrp/chgrp.c - this
page documents both, the arrangement rm(1) has with *rmdir*.

**Note:** there is no chgrp(2). The group is the third argument of chown(2), which has no
`leave this one alone` sentinel, so *chown* reads the group back out of a stat(2) and writes it
again, and *chgrp* does the same with the owner. Each therefore rewrites the field it is not
changing, and a program that changes both must be prepared for either order.

**Note:** the names available on the root filesystem this port ships are **root** (0),
**daemon** (1), **sys** (2), **bin** (3), **uucp** (4) and **guest** (7); the groups are
**other** (1), **sys** (2), **bin** (3) and **uucp** (4). There is no group with gid 0, so
**chgrp 0** must be written numerically.

**Note:** v7's programs accepted an empty first argument as a number, so **chown '' file**
handed the file to root without a word. It is refused here.

## DIAGNOSTICS

Both exit **4** for a usage error or an unknown name, and **1** if any file could not be
changed. The first two messages go to the standard **output,** which is v7's and is
inconsistent with the third; the third is perror(3) and goes to the standard error.

| **usage: chown uid file ...**
| **usage: chgrp gid file ...**

| **unknown user id:** *name*
| **unknown group:** *name*

**name**\&*:* **reason** - the file could not be *stat*\&ed or chown(2) refused it. For an
ordinary user the reason is **Not owner**.

## FILES

| /etc/passwd
| /etc/group

## SEE ALSO

chmod(1), chown(2), ls(1), passwd(5), group(5)
